INT. HARBOUR OFFICE - CONTINUOUS

Tide tables pinned to every wall. The HARBOURMASTER (60s) does not look up from his ledger.

NELL
Who mended my brother's boat?

HARBOURMASTER
Nobody mends anything round here, love. Tide does it all for us.

NELL
Someone nailed a plank.

HARBOURMASTER ^
Then someone wanted it to float.

A long silence. He finally closes the ledger.

HARBOURMASTER (CONT'D)
Your brother came in here the night before. Wet through. He asked me what time the water would be lowest, and I told him, because that's my job, and he wrote it on the back of his hand like a schoolboy, and then he asked me something I've thought about every night since, which was whether a man could walk to the island at low tide without anyone seeing him go, and I said yes, if he knew the sands, and if he didn't care whether he came back.
